   ],
   "l": "The people not fearing death,\nhow then frighten them with death?\nIf one made the people always fear death,\nand there were one who did the freakish thing,\nI could seize and kill him —\nwho would dare?",
   "v": true,
   "i": "If the people do not fear death, what is the use of frightening them with death? Suppose the people were made to fear death always, and someone did the freakish thing: I could seize him and put him to death — and then who would dare?",
   "n": "奈何 is the fixed question you met in chapter 26, 奈何萬乘之主: literally 'do what about ——?', and it always expects the answer that nothing can be done. 奈 hardly occurs outside this frame — in this book only here and in that chapter-26 line.\n\n懼 is causative by position alone. The word means 'to be afraid'; put an object after it and it means 'to make afraid', with no ending added and nothing marked. Chapter 72's 見 read xiàn did the same job the same way, except that there the change of word was audible.\n\nThe whole passage after the first line is a supposition, and the supposition is the argument. 若使 sets up something the text does not grant — that the people could be made to fear death always — and only inside that unreal frame does the ruler get his 'I could seize him and kill him'. 孰敢, 'who would dare', is the sound of a man enjoying the thought. The next unit takes the sword out of his hand.\n\nThis is one of the chapters whose text is least stable. The Mawangdui manuscripts are reported to open 若民恆且不畏死, 'if the people are constantly and certainly unafraid of death', and to differ again in the second half; the received wording is what is read here.",
   "p": true
  },
  {
