   "l": "There is always the one who has killing in charge, who does the killing.\nNow, to kill in place of the one who has killing in charge —\nthis is called hewing in place of the master carpenter.\nOf those who hew in place of the master carpenter,\nthere are few that do not gash their own hand.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "There is always one whose office the killing is, and he does it. To do the killing in his place is to do the master carpenter's hewing for him — and of those who take up the adze in the master carpenter's place, few come away without a cut in the hand.",
   "n": "The chapter never says who the 司殺者 is, and the commentary does not force it. Two candidates have always been available: the executioner, the officer whose actual work this is — 司 is an office word, 'to have charge of', and 有司 in classical prose simply means the officials — or heaven, the standing order of things, which kills without anybody deciding to. The second is the reading that gives the chapter its force, since a ruler can replace an officer but not the order of things; the first is what the words say. Both are on the table, and the English here keeps 'the one who has killing in charge' rather than choosing.\n\nWatch 者 do two different sizes of job in the same passage. In 司殺者 it closes a three-character phrase into a noun, 'the one who has charge of killing', and that noun is then what 代 replaces. In 夫代大匠斲者 it closes the whole of 代大匠斲 into a noun instead: 'the one who does the master carpenter's hewing for him'. Same character, same 'the one who ——', but nothing on the page shows you where the phrase it gathers began; only the sense does. 代 itself comes three times in five lines, and the repetition is doing the work of an argument: the substitution named, the substitution likened, the likeness pressed.\n\n大匠 is one tap-unit here rather than two. It is the standing term for the master carpenter — later even a real office title in the Han bureaucracy — and the 大 in it is not 'big' but the title-forming 'master-', which is exactly the test for binding a pair. 斲 is hewing with the adze, the rough squaring of timber, and the point of the image is the concrete danger of it. The hand is not a metaphor.\n\n希有不 X 矣, 'few there are that do not ——', says a strong thing by denying a weak one: nearly everyone who takes up the adze cuts himself. 希 is the chapter 14 word for what is too faint to be heard, used here of number. Chapter 70 had it too: 知我者希."
